WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.239
The king's own brother sealed a letter for a king that England

00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:04.551
had already buried.

00:00:04.951 --> 00:00:07.087
The brother was Edmund, Earl of Kent.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:11.035
The buried king was his half-brother, Edward the Second.

00:00:11.435 --> 00:00:14.082
Kent had stood at Edward's funeral at Gloucester.

00:00:14.482 --> 00:00:18.023
Just over two years later, Kent was beheaded for plotting to set

00:00:18.069 --> 00:00:19.219
his brother free.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.696
Why would a king's brother risk his life to rescue a man

00:00:22.742 --> 00:00:24.449
the whole kingdom had mourned?

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:35.362
Edmund of Kent was the youngest son of King Edward the First,

00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:38.195
born to the old king's second wife, and he came into the

00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:41.562
world when his half-brother Edward was already seventeen.

00:00:41.962 --> 00:00:45.503
By the time Kent was a grown man, that half-brother was king,

00:00:45.538 --> 00:00:46.885
as Edward the Second.

00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:48.887
Kent stayed loyal to him.

00:00:49.328 --> 00:00:52.904
Edward made him Earl of Kent, and for years Kent served him

00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:55.981
as one of the great lords of the realm, even in Gascony,

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:59.081
the English crown's lands in the south-west of France.

00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:02.093
But the reign Kent served was turning bitter.

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:06.749
Edward's favourites had turned England's barons against him, and soon the

00:01:06.819 --> 00:01:09.140
quarrel reached into the king's own family.

